<p>When a Port Arthur home or business is damaged by a hurricane, wind, fire, or water, the repair has to meet the building codes in force today, not the codes that applied when the building went up. In Jefferson County there is a second layer: repairs must also meet Texas Department of Insurance (TDI) windstorm requirements to stay eligible for Texas Windstorm Insurance Association (TWIA) coverage. That gap between "how it was built" and "how it must be rebuilt" is a frequent source of confusion on insurance claims. This page summarizes Port Arthur building codes and how they interact with insurance claims, using information published by the City of Port Arthur and TDI.</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout-title">Last verified: October 20, 2026</span><p>Codes, fees, and procedures change. This page is general information, not code consulting or legal advice. Always confirm current requirements with the City of Port Arthur Building Inspections Division before starting work.</p></div>

