<h2>TDI windstorm inspections and the WPI-8</h2>
<p>Jefferson County is a first-tier coastal county in the TWIA catastrophe area. Under the <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/wind/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TDI Windstorm Inspection Program</a>, structures, additions, and repairs in that area, <strong>including re-roofs and roof repairs</strong>, must be built and inspected to TDI's specifications to be eligible for TWIA coverage. When the work passes, TDI issues a <strong>WPI-8 Certificate of Compliance</strong>.</p>
<p>A few practical points for Port Arthur owners:</p>
<ul>
<li>Proof of enrollment in the TDI inspection program (a WPI-1) may be required before a building permit is issued in cities that adopted TDI's standard. Ask the City whether it applies to your job.</li>
<li>Windstorm compliance usually has to be planned before work starts, because inspections happen during construction. A roof that is covered before it is inspected can be hard to certify afterward.</li>
<li>Keep copies of WPI-8 certificates. They show the work met windstorm standards, which can matter for future TWIA coverage and future claims.</li>
<li>Windstorm-compliant installation methods and materials may cost more than the original construction. Whether your policy pays that difference depends on your coverage (see below).</li>
</ul>
<p>For more on how TWIA claims work, read our <a href="/twia-windstorm-claim-guide-port-arthur/">TWIA windstorm claim guide</a> or see <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a>.</p>

<h2>Roofing repair and replacement</h2>
<p>Roofs are where codes and claims meet most often in Port Arthur. Composition shingle roofs are common on homes, while metal roofs and low-slope roofs are typical on commercial and industrial buildings. When a roof is repaired or replaced after a hurricane or windstorm, the work generally needs a city permit, must meet the current IRC or IBC roofing provisions, and needs TDI windstorm inspection to keep TWIA eligibility.</p>
<p>Insurer estimates sometimes price the visible roofing material but leave out items that a permitted, windstorm-compliant installation requires. Items worth checking include underlayment type, drip edge, starter and ridge components, fastening, and flashing. Whether those items are owed depends on whether they are part of repairing covered damage under your policy and on any ordinance or law coverage. See <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a> and <a href="/services/hurricane-damage-claims/">hurricane damage claims</a>.</p>

<h2>Wind-resistant construction</h2>
<p>Port Arthur faces hurricane-force winds, tropical storms, and severe thunderstorms with straight-line winds. Wind design comes from the adopted IRC and IBC and the standards they reference, together with TDI's windstorm specifications for the catastrophe area. We do not list design wind speeds here; your designer, contractor, the City, or TDI can confirm the requirements for a particular project.</p>
<p>Remember that the City's hurricane flood protection levee and seawall system reduces storm surge risk but does not stop rainfall flooding, and flood damage is generally excluded from homeowners and TWIA policies. Code-related questions for flood repairs may involve your flood policy instead; see <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a>.</p>

<h2>Permit requirements</h2>
<p>Permits are handled by the Building Inspections Division. Re-roofing and structural repairs generally need a permit, and so do many electrical, plumbing, and mechanical repairs. We do not quote permit fees here; ask the permit techs for current fees. Permit costs for covered repairs are often part of a complete repair estimate, so keep copies of permits and fee receipts for your claim file.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Structural repairs</h2>
<p>Hurricane winds, fallen trees, fire, and long-term water damage can affect framing, trusses, walls, and foundations, including the piers and beams under many older Port Arthur homes. Structural repairs generally require permits, may require engineering, and must meet current code and TDI windstorm requirements. Repairing to current standards can cost more than restoring the original construction, which is where ordinance or law coverage becomes important.</p>

<h2>Code-related upgrades</h2>
<p>Code-related upgrades are items that are not "damaged" in the usual sense but must be added or changed because the repair triggers current requirements. Examples can include electrical updates under the 2020 NEC when walls are opened after water damage, or roofing and fastening components required by the 2021 IRC and TDI's windstorm specifications. Ask the City, TDI, or a licensed contractor which upgrades your project requires, and get that in writing where possible.</p>

<h2>Contractor responsibilities</h2>
<p>In most projects, the contractor pulls the permit, builds to code, arranges windstorm inspections, and schedules city inspections. Before work starts, ask your contractor to confirm the permit and WPI-8 process, explain which code items are included in the price, and itemize code-required work separately from like-kind repairs. That separation makes it easier to present code items to your insurer. Remember that in Texas a public adjuster may not take part in repairing a property on a claim they adjust, so your contractor and your public adjuster play different roles.</p>

<h2>Ordinance or law coverage</h2>
<p>Ordinance or law coverage pays, up to its limit, for increased costs to comply with building codes when repairing covered damage. It is often a separate, limited coverage or an endorsement, and the limit may be a percentage of the dwelling coverage or a fixed amount. Some policies have little or none. Check your declarations page and policy forms, including your TWIA policy, and note that some policies pay code-upgrade costs only after the work is actually completed.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warn"><span class="callout-title">Code compliance is not the same as coverage</span><p>A requirement under Port Arthur's codes or TDI's windstorm program does not automatically mean your insurer must pay for it. Payment for code upgrades depends on your policy's ordinance or law coverage, its limits, and its terms. Always read your own policy.</p></div>

<h2>Code-related claim disputes</h2>
<p>Disagreements often involve whether a code item was required, whether it relates to covered damage, or whether the policy includes enough ordinance or law coverage. Helpful steps include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Get written confirmation of the requirement from the City, TDI, or a licensed professional.</li>
<li>Ask your contractor to itemize code-required and windstorm-required work.</li>
<li>Compare the insurer's estimate line by line with the permitted scope.</li>
<li>Submit a <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplement</a> with the documentation, or request an <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">independent estimate review</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>If the dispute is over the amount of loss, your policy's <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal clause</a> may apply. For coverage disputes or legal deadlines, consult a Texas attorney.</p>

<h2>Confirm requirements with the City before work</h2>
<p>This page reflects our review of City and TDI sources as of October 20, 2026. Before starting any repair, contact the City of Port Arthur Building Inspections Division at (409) 983-8261 or visit the <a href="https://www.portarthurtx.gov/502/Inspections-and-Permitting" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Inspections and Permitting page</a>, and check TDI's <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/wind/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">windstorm inspection</a> requirements. If you have questions about how those requirements affect your claim, request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>.</p>
